# XSH sigsetjmp()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# sigsetjmp() is equivalent to setjmp() except for the saved mask. The
# mask's restoration is observable only through siglongjmp(); XSH.siglongjmp
# tests that rule, and the same observation is made here from sigsetjmp()'s
# side.

assertion  XSH.sigsetjmp.001
anchor     functions/sigsetjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sigsetjmp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       A direct call of sigsetjmp() returns 0, with savemask 0 and with
           savemask 1, and siglongjmp() then makes it return a non-zero
           value, once per siglongjmp().
test       t/001-direct-and-jump.c

assertion  XSH.sigsetjmp.002
anchor     functions/sigsetjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       sigsetjmp() works in the contexts setjmp() allows (if, switch,
           while controlling expressions, compared with a constant, under
           !, as an expression statement), the direct return taking the 0
           path and a siglongjmp() the other.
test       t/002-contexts.c

assertion  XSH.sigsetjmp.003
anchor     functions/sigsetjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With savemask non-zero, sigsetjmp() saves the calling thread's
           signal mask: a mask change after the call (blocking SIGUSR1,
           unblocking SIGUSR2) is undone by siglongjmp(), giving the mask at
           the time of the call.
test       t/003-saves-mask.c
